[38;5;231m⏺[39m Got it — I'll design so that decision can be deferred, behind an interface that lets the identity source be swapped
  without touching consumers. That has a real cost, and I'll name it in the design: a deferred identity source means
  every consumer must treat the id as [3mopaque and possibly untrustworthy[0m until the source is settled, so anything that
  needs to trust it stays blocked.

  Which makes this the next question, and it's the one that decides how expensive the deferral actually is. The fork:
  whether anything will [1mmake a trust decision[0m based on this id, or whether it's purely descriptive.

  - [1mDescriptive only[0m (analytics, logs, attribution, prefilling a form): a client-minted id is genuinely adequate,
    deferral is nearly free, and swapping in a server id later is a migration of stored data rather than a redesign.
  - [1mTrust-bearing[0m (deciding what a user may see or do, keying anything private, anything an attacker would benefit from
    forging): a client-minted id is never acceptable, because the browser can set it to any value. Deferral here is
    expensive — it means the real design can't be finished, and I'd want to build only the descriptive slice now and
    explicitly fence it off from authorization.

  The distinction matters more than it sounds: a "track who logged in" id that starts as descriptive very often gets
  quietly reused for a trust decision later, and that's the failure this question is trying to prevent.
